document MdsMarkdownSchema

# "MDS — Markdown Document Schema Specification" as title required

# Indent required

embed mds

expect:
  Show the mds code example declaring a required "## Purpose" section with required prose of at least 50 characters
  and its accompanying expect: block instructing the author to explain why the component exists.
  Do not include unrelated schema keywords not shown in this example.

validate:
  semantic: optional

embed text

expect:
  Explain that the structural part of an MDS contract is deterministic and testable, that expect is native semantic
  guidance a human, LLM or agent can read while generating the document, and that a semantic-validation extension
  MAY evaluate that expectation.

validate:
  semantic: optional

embed text

expect:
  Show the text diagram tracing task + .mds contract through human/LLM/agent, Markdown document, MDS structural
  validation, to optional semantic validation.

validate:
  semantic: optional

embed mermaid

expect:
  Show the mermaid flowchart connecting MDS Contract and Task/Intent into Human/LLM/Agent, producing a Markdown
  Document that an MDS Validator checks, branching to Accepted or Diagnostics/Repair, with repair feeding back
  to the author.

validate:
  semantic: optional

# Why MDS Matters required

## 1. LLM Output Becomes Testable required

list string minItems=9

embed text

expect:
  List the LLM failure modes this section names: omitting sections, renaming headings, inventing extra sections,
  producing malformed tables, forgetting required fields, generating invalid Mermaid, returning incomplete JSON,
  breaking requested ordering, and ignoring subtle formatting requirements.

validate:
  semantic: optional

embed text

expect:
  Show the text diagram tracing Prompt plus MDS contract through the LLM to Markdown, then MDS validation, to a
  PASS/FAIL outcome, and explain that expect keeps semantic generation intent alongside structural rules instead
  of a separate prompt template.

validate:
  semantic: optional

# 2. Documentation Becomes Executable required

list string minItems=6

embed text

expect:
  List the ways organizations maintain documentation standards informally, as named here: templates, examples,
  wiki pages, style guides, prompt instructions and tribal knowledge, and state that these are merely advisory.

validate:
  semantic: optional

embed text

expect:
  Show the informal text list of required engine document sections (Purpose, Inputs, Outputs, Dependencies, Risks)
  that MDS replaces with an executable engine.mds contract.

validate:
  semantic: optional

embed bash

expect:
  Show the CLI invocation `mds validate engine.md engine.mds` used by CI to enforce the schema.

validate:
  semantic: optional

embed text

expect:
  Show the mapping list contrasting engineered artifacts and their contracts: Code to tests, API to OpenAPI
  contract, Database to schema, and Markdown docs to MDS contract.

validate:
  semantic: optional

# 3. Markdown Keeps Its Biggest Advantage required

embed markdown

expect:
  Show the Signal Engine markdown example with a Purpose section and an Inputs table listing Name, Type and
  Source columns for price and volume rows.

validate:
  semantic: optional

embed mds

expect:
  Show the matching mds schema for the Signal Engine example: a wildcard title, a required Purpose section with
  required prose, and a required Inputs table with required Name, Type and Source string columns.

validate:
  semantic: optional

# 4. Structured Documents Without JSON/XML Noise required

list string minItems=7

embed text

expect:
  List the document constructs markdown handles more naturally than JSON/YAML/XML, as named here: prose,
  headings, tables, diagrams, code, examples and nested explanations, and show the JSON/YAML versus Markdown
  versus Markdown+MDS contrast diagram.

validate:
  semantic: optional

# 5. Recursive Multi-Format Validation required

embed markdown

expect:
  Show the markdown example with nested json, mermaid and latex fenced code blocks under Configuration,
  Architecture and Formula sections.

validate:
  semantic: optional

embed mds

expect:
  Show the matching mds schema declaring the Configuration, Architecture and Formula sections with embed json,
  embed mermaid and embed latex declarations of the appropriate cardinality.

validate:
  semantic: optional

embed mermaid

expect:
  Show the mermaid diagram in which MDS Core delegates to JSON, Mermaid and LaTeX extensions, each routing to
  its own external validator.

validate:
  semantic: optional

# 6. The Document Can Describe Its Own Contract Requirements required

list string minItems=7

embed text

expect:
  List the introspection questions a schema can answer, as named here: which sections are required, which may
  repeat, which tables are expected, which columns are mandatory, which fields are typed, which embedded formats
  occur, which external schemas are required, and which validators are needed, plus the list of things tooling
  can auto-generate from that model.

validate:
  semantic: optional

# 7. One Contract for Human and Machine Authors required

embed text

expect:
  List the systems that can share the same schema, as named here: human author, LLM, agent, IDE, CI pipeline,
  documentation generator and validator.

validate:
  semantic: optional

embed text

expect:
  Show the ASCII diagram of architecture.mds feeding Human, LLM and IDE, all producing architecture.md, which
  the MDS Validator checks, illustrating one source of truth.

validate:
  semantic: optional

# 8. MDS Adds Contracts Without Destroying Markdown required

prose required minLength=326

expect:
  Explain that MDS adds contracts without turning Markdown into XML, without wrapping every paragraph, without JSON-like brackets and without YAML indentation for the document itself, closing with the quoted design goal of keeping the document optimized for humans while adding a separate formal contract optimized for machines.

validate:
  semantic: optional

# 9. MDS Is a Document Schema, Not Just a Data Schema required

embed text

expect:
  List the document-oriented constructs MDS can express, as named here: section hierarchy, section ordering,
  required prose, optional prose, repeated sections, tables, human labels, structured fields, nested content,
  embedded languages and document composition.

validate:
  semantic: optional

embed text

expect:
  List the data-oriented constraints MDS can express, as named here: string, integer, number, boolean, date,
  enum, array, object, required, optional, min/max, pattern and unique.

validate:
  semantic: optional

# 10. Positioning required

embed text

expect:
  Show the positioning analogy list: OOXML/DOCX plus formal document schemas, ODF/ODT plus formal document
  schemas, and Markdown plus MDS, followed by JSON plus JSON Schema and XML plus XSD for data-oriented formats.

validate:
  semantic: optional

embed text

expect:
  Show the two blockquoted positioning statements: that MDS is to Markdown what formal schema languages are to
  structured XML documents, and that prompts describe what an LLM should produce while MDS proves whether the
  result conforms.

validate:
  semantic: optional

# 11. Key Differentiators required

prose required minLength=1508

expect:
  Present the comparison table of Plain Markdown, JSON/YAML schemas and MDS across the listed capabilities (human-readable prose, headings/sections, structured fields, typed values, required sections, section order, tables as typed records, embedded languages, external validator delegation, recursive schema validation, LLM generation contract, deterministic document validation, introspection and human-first authoring), showing MDS checked for every row.

validate:
  semantic: optional

# 12. Primary Use Cases required

embed text

expect:
  List the primary use cases named here: architecture documents, technical specifications, ADRs, design
  documents, runbooks, operating procedures, research reports, experiment reports, API documentation,
  data-source documentation, engine specifications, system design documents, AI-generated reports, agent
  outputs, knowledge-base documents, compliance documentation and documentation CI.

validate:
  semantic: optional

# Technical Specification required

prose required minLength=322

expect:
  State the RFC 2119 conformance language for this specification: MUST/MUST NOT apply to conforming implementations, SHOULD indicates a strong recommendation requiring justification for deviation, and MAY marks a genuinely optional capability.

validate:
  semantic: optional

# 13. Core Definition required

embed text

expect:
